faq:
  - q: "What is the Power Law floor?"
    a: "It is the lower edge of the Power Law channel &mdash; a line drawn at 0.42&times; the trend price, using the same exponent, so it runs parallel to the trend rather than converging on it. It is not a support level and nothing enforces it. It is the level the model nominates as its own worst case: the place the Power Law has agreed to be judged."
  - q: "Has the floor ever broken?"
    a: "Yes &mdash; once seriously, and three times barely. In 2010 price closed 42.6% below the floor and stayed under for 48 days, in an illiquid market that predates every exchange we would now call mature. The other three approaches were grazes: 1.8% under in August 2015, 5.1% under that September, and 0.4% under in January 2023, all of them single prints or a few weeks. Reported as &ldquo;the floor has broken four times&rdquo; those two facts get flattened into one, which is why this page keeps them apart."
  - q: "Does buying at the floor beat the model?"
    a: "Measured at the floor, no &mdash; and that is the more useful finding. Entries within 10% of the floor returned an enormous absolute CAGR, but graded to a day when price was back on the floor, their excess over the trend&rsquo;s own growth across the identical window was approximately zero. The extra return came from how far <em>below</em> the line you bought, not from buying at it. The answer depends entirely on where you stop measuring &mdash; the instrument on this page recomputes it live and shows both endpoints, so the figure you see may differ from the published one depending on where price sits today."
  - q: "What would prove the Power Law wrong?"
    a: "A sustained break of the floor. The page publishes the parameters it is watching &mdash; a close more than 10% below the floor, held for more than 30 consecutive days &mdash; along with a live status line, so the test is stated in advance rather than rationalised afterwards. A break of the ceiling would matter too, for a different reason: it would suggest the denominator, not the model, had changed."
