  <!-- ══════════ 1 · WHAT THE FLOOR IS ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="what-the-floor-is">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The line</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">What the floor is</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">The floor is not a support level and nothing enforces it. It is the level the model nominates as its own worst case &mdash; and a model that names its worst case can be checked against it.</p>

    <div class="fl-prose">
      <p>The Power Law trend is a line through bitcoin&rsquo;s price history in log-log space. The floor is that same line, shifted down: <strong>0.42&times; the trend price, at the same exponent</strong>. Because the exponent is shared, the two run parallel forever rather than converging &mdash; the floor is a statement about <em>level</em>, not about slope. That is a property of how the channel is defined, not something measured.</p>

      <p>What <em>can</em> be measured is whether the price history&rsquo;s actual lower edge runs parallel to the line we drew. It does, and it sits slightly above it.</p>

      <p>Fitting the lower edge independently &mdash; quantile regressions at the 2nd through 10th percentiles, each free to choose its own slope &mdash; produces exponents clustered in a narrow band of <strong>5.86 to 5.96</strong>, against the canonical trend exponent of <strong>5.77</strong>. Every quantile from the 2nd to the median lands between 5.79 and 5.96, which is real support for the <em>form</em> of the model: a constant-multiple channel, not one that fans open or shut with time.</p>

      <p>The second finding is the one that matters for a reader standing at the floor. Forcing the canonical slope, the 2nd-percentile line implies a multiple of <strong>0.4209&times;</strong> &mdash; almost exactly the 0.42 the site draws. A true 5th-percentile floor on this record would sit near <strong>0.46&times;</strong>, roughly 10% higher than our line. <strong>The floor is stricter than &ldquo;5% of observations below&rdquo;</strong>: it is closer to a 2nd-percentile line. History hugged a line above where we drew ours, and the residual errs in the reader&rsquo;s favour rather than the model&rsquo;s.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-chart-block">
      <div class="fl-chart-head">
        <div>
          <div class="fl-chart-title" id="flChannelTitle">Bitcoin against the Power Law channel</div>
          <div class="fl-chart-sub" id="flChannelSub">Log-log. The floor is drawn solid; trend and upper band are secondary. Shaded bands mark the four approaches to the floor.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-chart-wrapper chart-wrapper" data-chart-copy
           data-chart-heading="#flChannelTitle"
           data-chart-sub="#flChannelSub"
           data-chart-title="The Bitcoin Floor — the channel, floor emphasised"
