        <canvas id="flChannelChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <p class="fl-chart-note">Hover any point for trend, floor and price together. <span class="fl-tip" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-describedby="flTipExp">Why the floor&rsquo;s exponent is the trend&rsquo;s<span class="fl-tip-body" id="flTipExp" role="tooltip">The floor is defined as a constant multiple of trend, so its exponent is 5.77 by construction &mdash; not by fit. Fitted independently, the record&rsquo;s lower edge comes out slightly steeper: 5.8834 at the 5th percentile, 5.9552 at the 2nd, and 5.8999 through the eight cycle lows. Three methods, all near 5.88&ndash;5.96. Over sixteen years that gap is small; extrapolated forward it compounds in log space, which is the honest caveat on any long projection.</span></span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 2 · EPISODE EXPLORER ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="the-four-approaches">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The record</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">Four times price has come here</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">Four times price has come to the floor. What happened next is the record &mdash; not a promise. One of the four is not like the others, and averaging them together is the main way this evidence gets misused.</p>

    <div class="fl-prose">
      <p><strong>One real break, three grazes.</strong> In 2010 price closed <strong>42.6% below the floor</strong> and stayed under for 48 days. The other three went under by 1.8%, 5.1% and 0.4%, and two of those were single prints. A sentence like &ldquo;the floor has broken four times&rdquo; puts a 42.6% break in bitcoin&rsquo;s illiquid infancy in the same bucket as a 0.4% touch in 2023. This page keeps them apart.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-ep-strip" id="flEpStrip" role="tablist" aria-label="Floor episodes"></div>
    <div class="fl-ep-card" id="flEpCard" role="tabpanel" aria-live="polite"></div>

    <div class="fl-stat-row" id="flRevStats">
      <div class="fl-stat">
        <div class="fl-stat-v" id="flRevMedian">&mdash;</div>
        <div class="fl-stat-k">median gap to trend closed within 24 months</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-stat">
        <div class="fl-stat-v" id="flRevOvershoot">&mdash;</div>
        <div class="fl-stat-k">of the four closed the gap entirely and overshot past trend</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-stat">
        <div class="fl-stat-v">4</div>
        <div class="fl-stat-k">observations &mdash; an ordering of four numbers, not a distribution</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fl-caveat">At <strong>12 months</strong> the four share no shape at all &mdash; the outcomes run from 11% of the gap closed to several hundred percent, and the median of that spread should not be used. At 24 months they agree far better, and that is the most defensible statement available here. It still rests on four observations, in a series where the model&rsquo;s own coefficients were fitted on substantially this same history.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 3 · THE PARITY INSTRUMENT ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="what-the-floor-paid">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The measurement</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">What entering at the floor has paid</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">The same entries, two honest endpoints, two different stories &mdash; and both are true. What &ldquo;did the floor pay&rdquo; means depends entirely on where you stop measuring, so this instrument makes you choose.</p>

    <div class="fl-endpoint-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Measurement endpoint">
      <span class="fl-endpoint-label">Measure to:</span>
      <button type="button" class="fl-seg-btn is-active" data-endpoint="today" aria-pressed="true">To today (the floor)</button>
      <button type="button" class="fl-seg-btn" data-endpoint="trend" aria-pressed="false">To the last trend touch</button>
    </div>

    <div class="fl-parity-card" id="flParityCard">
      <div class="fl-parity-grid">
        <div class="fl-parity-cell">
          <div class="fl-parity-k">Realized CAGR</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-v" id="flParityRealized">&mdash;</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-sub" id="flParityRealizedSub">median across the entries</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fl-parity-cell">
          <div class="fl-parity-k">Trend CAGR, same windows</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-v" id="flParityTrend">&mdash;</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-sub">what the model itself grew at</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fl-parity-cell fl-parity-cell-excess">
          <div class="fl-parity-k">Excess over the model</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-v" id="flParityExcess">&mdash;</div>
          <div class="fl-parity-sub" id="flParityExcessSub">median of the per-entry differences</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fl-parity-method" id="flParityMethod">&mdash;</p>

      <div class="fl-parity-read" id="flParityRead"></div>

      <div class="fl-honesty">
        <div class="fl-honesty-item">
          <div class="fl-honesty-k">The vintage caveat</div>
          <p id="flHonestyVintage">These CAGRs are enormous because the median entry dates from bitcoin&rsquo;s higher-growth past. The model&rsquo;s own arithmetic declines with age: an entry at the floor today implies far less forward growth than an entry at the floor in 2015 did, and nothing here should be read as the rate a floor entry would earn from here.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="fl-honesty-item">
          <div class="fl-honesty-k">The endpoint caveat</div>
          <p id="flHonestyEndpoint">&mdash;</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="fl-parity-foot">Excess is the median of each entry&rsquo;s own realized-minus-trend difference &mdash; not the difference of the two medians above, which is a different and less meaningful number. Windows run from each entry date to the selected endpoint; CAGRs annualise on a 365.25-day year.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 4 · THE TRIPWIRE ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="the-tripwire">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">The falsifier</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">The tripwire</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">A thesis that names its falsifier is a different kind of object than a prediction. These are the numbers, published in advance.</p>

    <div class="fl-tripwire" id="flTripwire">
      <div class="fl-tripwire-head">The test this model has agreed to</div>
      <p class="fl-tripwire-crit">The Power Law is failing its own test if price sustains a close <strong>more than 10% below the floor</strong> for <strong>more than 30 consecutive days</strong>.</p>
      <p class="fl-tripwire-why">Those parameters are chosen to separate the one real break from the three grazes. The grazes never came close to them: the deepest went 5.1% under, and none held. The 2010 break exceeded both, on every measure available in this series.</p>

      <div class="fl-tripwire-status" id="flTripwireStatus">
        <div class="fl-tripwire-status-k">Status today</div>
        <div class="fl-tripwire-status-v" id="flTripwireStatusV">&mdash;</div>
      </div>

      <p class="fl-tripwire-ceiling">Secondary watch, the other direction: a sustained break <em>above</em> the 3&times; ceiling, beyond the brief cyclical visits the record already contains, would not falsify the model so much as suggest the denominator had changed &mdash; that the thing being priced, or the money pricing it, is no longer the same measurement.</p>

      <p class="fl-tripwire-commit">If it fires, this page will say so &mdash; the same day, in this box.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ 5 · THE OTHER SIDE ══════════ -->
  <section class="fl-section" id="the-other-side">
    <div class="fl-eyebrow">Both sides</div>
    <h2 class="fl-h2">The other side of this</h2>
    <p class="fl-lead">Four reasons to hold everything above more loosely than the precision of the numbers suggests.</p>

    <div class="fl-otherside">
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">The 2010 break is real, and it is not an asterisk</div>
        <p>The floor&rsquo;s record starts with a failure: 42.6% under, for 48 days. The honest qualifier is that it happened in a market with no mature exchange, negligible liquidity and a price measured in cents &mdash; conditions that no longer obtain. That qualifier is a reason to weight it differently, not a reason to drop it, which is why every count on this page says &ldquo;four&rdquo; and then immediately says which one was different.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">If adoption saturates, the floor breaks before anything else does</div>
        <p>The strongest argument against the Power Law is that it describes an adoption curve which must eventually flatten &mdash; an S-curve caught mid-rise looks like a power law right up until it doesn&rsquo;t. The tripwire is exactly the instrument that would register saturation as it began to bite, because a flattening adoption curve shows up first as a floor that stops holding. See <a href="/the-power-law">the Power Law page</a> for the model&rsquo;s limits in full.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">A floor test can fail for denominator reasons</div>
        <p>Every figure here is a ratio with dollars underneath it. A break of the floor would be evidence about bitcoin; it could also be evidence about the dollar, or about the price series, or about which exchanges the series draws from. The test is not clean, and no single test of a monetary thesis is.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-os-item">
        <div class="fl-os-k">Nothing here is an entry signal</div>
        <p>&ldquo;At the floor&rdquo; is a location, not a verdict about value. The measurement above found that entering there did not beat the model, and the sample it found that in is four episodes deep. Position size is the tool that survives being wrong about all of this &mdash; see <a href="/bitcoin-allocation-sizing">Bitcoin Portfolio Allocation</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

